course: dictionary
lesson: 11639
title: Arousal: awake, alert and ready
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Arousal is the state of being awake, alert or excited, when your body and mind are ready for action. You feel it when a loud bang makes you sit up straight at once.
explain: In science, arousal means that the brain and body become active. «When the dog saw its ball, its heart beat faster and it was wide awake.» The faster heartbeat and the wide-awake dog both show arousal.
explain: Sport uses the idea too. «Before the final, Mia felt so nervous that her hands shook, but once she settled down, she played brilliantly.» Players need the right level of arousal: not too sleepy, and not too nervous.
fact: Arousal means being awake, alert or excited, and ready for action.
fact: Arousal is a noun, so you can talk about a high level of arousal or a low level of arousal.
fact: When you are scared or excited, your heart can beat faster, and that is a sign of arousal in your body.
fact: Everyday words that are close in meaning include excitement and being alert, but arousal is the science word.
fact: Too little arousal can make you sleepy, while too much can make you too nervous to think clearly.
exercise: Arousal means being awake, alert or excited. Which is the best example? | Lying still and calm | Fast asleep in bed | *Sitting up straight when a loud bang makes you alert | Yawning all afternoon
exercise: «When the lights went out, my heart began to thump and I was wide awake.» Which word shows that the body is active? | out | went | lights | *thump
exercise: In science, what happens when the brain and body become active? | The body falls into a deep sleep | Nothing at all happens | The heart stops beating | *Arousal rises, and the body gets ready for action
test: Why might a footballer need the right level of arousal before a match? | *To feel alert and ready, but not too nervous | To fall asleep before kick-off | To make the heart stop beating | To forget the rules of the game
test: Which sentence shows a LOW level of arousal? | *«Tom yawned, his eyelids were heavy, and he could barely stay awake.» | «The alarm sounded and Ben jumped up at once.» | «The crowd roared and the whole stadium shook.» | «Lisa cheered and waved her arms in the air.»
exam: Which everyday words are closest in meaning to arousal? | Cold and wet | *Excitement and being alert | Sleepiness and boredom | Heavy and slow
